// @check
// @error argument 1: expected (f64, f64) -> f64, got (i32, _) -> i32
// The BOUNDARY of the partial-annotation value path, and the reason the rest of it is sound.
// `mpadd` writes `a: i32`; the callback parameter declares `(f64, f64) => f64`. Filling the hole
// `b` from the boundary would ALSO have to re-read the written `a: i32` as `f64` — inventing a
// signature the author did not write — so the two are simply not assignable and the CHECKER says
// so, naming the hole as `_` in the function type it inferred.
//
// The emitter holds the same line independently: its instance materializer requires an
// already-annotated parameter to AGREE with the boundary's, and declines otherwise, so a shape
// that reached emit with this disagreement would meet the value floor rather than a wrong pin.
//
// The accepting twin of every other cell in this family is
// `generic-fn-value-partial-annotation.vl`; the only difference is whether the written annotation
// AGREES. A fully un-annotated `mpadd` IS accepted here and pinned at (f64, f64) — the annotation
// is what constrains it, which is the direction an annotation is supposed to work in.
function mpadd(a: i32, b) { return a + b }
function applyMF(g: (f64, f64) => f64) { return g(1.5, 2.5) }

print(applyMF(mpadd))
